  "unit.v": "{v} V",
  "unit.ratio": "{a} : {b}",
  "unit.kw": "{v} kW",
  "unit.ii": "{a} A · {b} A",
  "ch5.say.down": "Turns ratio {ratio}: secondary voltage is 1/{n} of primary, about <em>{v2} V</em>. Power stays equal, so secondary current is {n}× primary: {i1} A in, {i2} A out. Stepping voltage down trades voltage for current.",
  "ch5.say.up": "Turns ratio {ratio}: secondary voltage is {n}× primary, about <em>{v2} V</em>, while secondary current drops to 1/{n}. Step-up transformers outside power plants do this in reverse of neighborhood ones: trading current for voltage to slash Chapter 4's line loss.",
  "ch5.say.same": "Turns ratio 1 : 1: equal voltage and equal current on both sides — pure electrical isolation.",
  "ch5.say.dc": "Switched to DC: at turn-on the magnetic field rises briefly, giving one pulse on the secondary; once steady, flux stops changing and <em>secondary voltage is 0</em>. Only the primary winding's small resistance limits current, which overheats a real transformer. That is why DC could not be stepped up a century ago.",
  "ch5.p3": "This is an <strong>ideal transformer</strong>: V₂/V₁ = N₂/N₁, I₁/I₂ = N₂/N₁, and power in equals power out. The default 26 : 1 ratio steps 10 kV down to ~385 V; supplying a 100 kW load draws ~10 A on the primary and ~260 A on the secondary. China's low-voltage distribution standard is 220/380 V (GB/T 156): 220 V from one phase wire to neutral, 380 V between two phase wires — the next chapter explains that √3 factor.",
  "ch5.p4": "AC won the nineteenth-century distribution battle, yet DC never vanished. Modern power-electronics converter stations rectify AC to DC and invert it back, powering <strong>high-voltage direct current (HVDC)</strong> lines over extreme distances, undersea cables, and links between unsynchronized grids (a trend the same DOE overview highlights). Saying “AC won” refers to the 1890s contest and the transformer-based distribution that still ends at your wall outlet.",
  "ch5.limit": "An ideal transformer has zero core loss, zero winding resistance, no flux leakage, and no core saturation. Real utility transformers are highly efficient, though core and copper losses still exist; no single representative efficiency figure is claimed here without a checked reference.",

  /* ───── 06 ───── */
  "ch6.title": "Why Three Wires Are Enough",
  "ch6.law": "Three AC waves spaced 120° apart sum to zero at every instant, and their combined instantaneous power stays flat at every instant.",
  "ch6.p1": "Look up at high-voltage pylons and you usually see wires in threes. Inside a generator sit three sets of windings spaced 120° around the stator, producing three identical sine waves staggered by a third of a cycle — phases A, B, and C. Why three, and why 120°?",
  "ch6.p2": "Single-phase AC power pulses up and down: every time voltage crosses zero — 100 times a second at 50 Hz — power drops to zero. Light bulbs do not care, while heavy industrial motors feel torque shudder. Three phases behave differently; add them yourself below.",
  "ch6.model.title": "Three-phase power: add the waves yourself",
  "ch6.model.how": "Drag “Instant (phase angle)” to read all three phases and their sum (dashed white) at that moment. Green below shows total three-phase power across three equal resistors. Then change “Phase spacing” from 120° to 90°, 60°, or 0°.",
  "ch6.model.aria1": "Three-phase voltage waveforms and their instantaneous sum",
  "ch6.model.aria2": "Total instantaneous three-phase power",
