  "ch6.read.vals": "A · B · C right now",
  "ch6.read.sum": "Instant sum",
  "ch6.read.ripple": "Total power ripple",
  "ch6.read.line": "Line ÷ phase voltage",
  "ch6.cv.x": "Phase angle (deg)",
  "ch6.cv.y": "Voltage (peak = 1)",
  "ch6.cv.p": "Total power",
  "unit.deg": "{v}°",
  "unit.vals": "{a} · {b} · {c}",
  "ch6.say.120": "120° spacing: right now {a} + {b} + {c} = <em>{s}</em>. Drag anywhere and the sum stays 0, so the three wires serve as each other's return path with no separate return conductor needed; total power is a flat line with 0% ripple.",
  "ch6.say.60": "60° spacing: total power is still flat ({rip}% ripple), yet the three waves no longer cancel — right now the sum is {s}, peaking at {nmax}, requiring a hefty return wire. Only 120° delivers both.",
  "ch6.say.zero": "0° spacing: all three phases stack into one triple-thick wire. Total power ripples {rip}%, dropping to zero twice per cycle, while neutral current peaks at {nmax}.",
  "ch6.say.other": "{phi}° spacing: total power ripples ({rip}%), and the sum is {s} now (peaking at {nmax}), requiring a return conductor.",
  "ch6.p3": "This rests on two trigonometric identities: sin²θ + sin²(θ − 120°) + sin²(θ − 240°) = 3/2 and sinθ + sin(θ − 120°) + sin(θ − 240°) = 0. The first says total power is constant; the second says balanced currents cancel completely at the return node, so three wires suffice (low-voltage street feeders add a fourth neutral wire to carry residential imbalance). In single-phase power, instantaneous output swings between 0 and twice the mean — a 200% ripple. Notice 60°: power is constant too, yet the currents fail to cancel.",
  "ch6.p4": "Voltage between two phases equals 2·sin(spacing/2) times phase-to-neutral voltage, which at 120° is √3 ≈ 1.732. That is why China's 220 V outlet (phase-to-neutral) and 380 V industrial supply (phase-to-phase) are the exact same system: 220 × 1.732 ≈ 381. Factory motors take all three phases at 380 V; your wall socket taps one phase at 220 V.",
  "ch6.limit": "Loads here are purely resistive and perfectly balanced. Real residential neighborhoods never balance perfectly, sending modest current down the neutral; inductive loads such as motors also shift current phase relative to voltage (power factor), omitted here.",

  /* ───── 07 ───── */
  "ch7.title": "Who Dispatches First: Merit Order",
  "ch7.law": "You decide how much power is used, while a cost queue decides who generates it: cheapest plants run first, and the last plant called sets the price for the whole hour.",
  "ch7.p1": "In Chapter 3 you controlled only “total generation”. In reality a grid holds hundreds of units whose marginal cost to generate one extra megawatt-hour (mostly fuel) varies widely. Wind and sun pay zero for fuel; nuclear fuel is cheap though plants prefer steady output; gas plants track natural-gas prices; oil and peakers cost the most and run only near peaks.",
  "ch7.p2": "Many electricity markets clear via a simple rule: line every generator up from lowest to highest marginal cost, and call them in order until demand is met. The final plant called is the <strong>marginal unit</strong>, and its cost becomes the clearing price paid to everyone generating that hour. This ranking is the <strong>merit order</strong>.",
  "ch7.model.title": "A toy electricity market",
  "ch7.model.how": "Top: generators stacked by cost — width is available capacity, height is cost per MWh, and the white line is demand for that hour. Drag “Hour” through the day to watch prices move, and drag “Natural gas price” to see plants swap places in the queue. Bottom: clearing price across all 24 hours.",
  "ch7.model.aria1": "Merit-order supply stack and current hour demand",
  "ch7.model.aria2": "24-hour clearing price bar chart",
  "ch7.ctl.hour": "Hour",
  "ch7.ctl.gas": "Natural gas price",
